\description{
Builds the standard station table of EarthFlux: the metadata of the
weather stations (\code{stations}, one row per station) and their
observations (\code{observations}, one row per station and timestamp), joined
by \code{station_id}, together with the provenance of the data. The
constructor normalises the inputs (plain data frames, timestamps in UTC,
declared unit conversions), removes exact duplicated records and
validates the contract. It never guesses units or time zones and never
corrects values: impossible metadata are errors, while impossible or
suspicious observations are kept and flagged by \code{\link[=ef_station_qc]{ef_station_qc()}}.
}
\details{
\strong{Time} (dev/specs/weather-forcing.md section 2.4). Timestamps are
stored as \code{POSIXct} in UTC. A \code{POSIXct} input must carry an explicit time
zone (a non-empty \code{tzone} attribute); character input is parsed with the
declared \code{timestamp_format} and \code{tz}, and any string that does not parse
(including local times that do not exist because of a daylight saving
change) is an error. Local times that occur twice at the end of daylight
saving time are resolved by \code{\link[=as.POSIXct]{as.POSIXct()}}; to avoid the ambiguity give
UTC or a fixed-offset zone such as \code{"Etc/GMT+4"}. For averages over a
period the mid-point is \code{timestamp - interval / 2} (\code{"end"}) or
\code{timestamp + interval / 2} (\code{"start"}); FAO-56 Eq. 31 uses the time at
the mid-point of the period.

\strong{Duplicates.} Records with the same \code{station_id} and \code{timestamp} and
identical values are kept once and counted in
\code{provenance$duplicates_removed}; duplicates with different values are an
error (they are never averaged).

\strong{Domain.} Impossible metadata are errors of class
\code{earthflux_domain_error}: coordinates out of range, non-finite
elevation, heights at or below their bound. Infinite observed values are
also errors. Physically impossible observations (e.g. a negative
relative humidity) are not errors here: they are kept and flagged with
severity \code{critical} by \code{\link[=ef_station_qc]{ef_station_qc()}} (decision WF-04), so that one
record does not stop a long series.

\strong{Errors.} All errors inherit \code{earthflux_input_error}. Specific classes:
\code{earthflux_station_field_error} (missing, unknown or mistyped field, zero
rows, missing value in a required field), \code{earthflux_station_time_error}
(timestamp without explicit time zone, unparsable timestamp, invalid
\code{timestamp_convention} or \code{interval}), \code{earthflux_station_unknown_id}
(observations of a station not in \code{stations}),
\code{earthflux_station_duplicate} (duplicated \code{station_id} in \code{stations},
or duplicated records with different values),
\code{earthflux_station_provenance_error} (provenance not a named list,
missing or unsupported \code{crs}, reserved fields) and
\code{earthflux_domain_error}.

Not covered yet: readers for the files or services of specific networks
(their access, licences, time zones and sensor heights are open items,
dev/specs/weather-forcing.md section 9) and the vertical datum of the
elevation.
